    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Non-Communicable Diseases in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p>Non-communicable diseases (NCDs) are<strong> long-term health conditions</strong> that cannot spread from person to person.</p><p>Unlike infections (e.g., malaria or flu), NCDs develop slowly and are often linked to lifestyle, genetics, or environmental factors.</p><p><strong>Examples of NCDs in Nigeria:</strong></p><ul><li>Hypertension (high blood pressure).</li><li>Diabetes.</li><li>Asthma.</li><li>Arthritis.</li><li>Cancer.</li></ul><h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Causes of Non-Communicable Diseases</strong></h2><p>NCDs arise from various factors:</p><p><strong>1. Genetic/Hereditary Factors</strong></p><ul><li>Inherited traits passed down from parents. <ul><li><em>Example:</em>Sickle cell anaemia (common in Nigeria).</li><li><em>Example:</em>Epilepsy (seizure disorders).</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>2. Lifestyle Choices</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Poor Diet:</strong> Eating too much processed food, salt, or sugary drinks. <ul><li><em>Example:</em>Diabetes from excessive sugar intake.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Lack of Exercise:</strong> Sedentary habits leading to obesity.</li><li><strong>Substance Abuse:</strong> Smoking or excessive alcohol use. <ul><li><em>Example:</em>Lung cancer from smoking.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>3. Environmental Factors</strong></p><ul><li>Exposure to harmful chemicals or pollution. <ul><li><em>Example:</em>Asthma triggered by air pollution in Lagos.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>4. Metabolic Disorders</strong></p><ul><li>Body’s inability to process nutrients properly. <ul><li><em>Example:</em>Goitre (iodine deficiency).</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>5. Congenital Conditions</strong></p><ul><li>Birth defects present from birth. <ul><li><em>Example:</em>Cleft lip (not "hare’s lip").</li><li><em>Example:</em>Cerebral palsy.</li></ul></li></ul><h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Common NCDs in Nigeria</strong></h1><ol start="1"><li><strong>Hypertension (High Blood Pressure):</strong><ul><li><strong>Causes:</strong> High salt intake, stress, obesity.</li><li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Headaches, dizziness.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Diabetes:</strong><ul><li><strong>Causes:</strong> Excess sugar consumption, lack of insulin.</li><li><strong>Symptoms:</strong> Frequent urination, thirst.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Asthma:</strong><ul><li><strong>Causes:</strong> Air pollution, dust, allergies.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Cancer:</strong><ul><li><strong>Causes:</strong> Smoking, radiation, unhealthy diets.</li></ul></li></ol><h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Prevention of NCDs</strong></h1><ol start="1"><li><strong>Healthy Eating:</strong><ul><li>Eat more fruits, vegetables, and whole grains.</li><li>Avoid fried foods and sugary drinks.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Regular Exercise:</strong><ul><li>Engage in 30 minutes of daily activity (e.g., walking, dancing).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Avoid Harmful Substances:</strong><ul><li>Say no to smoking and alcohol.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Regular Health Check-ups:</strong><ul><li>Monitor blood pressure and blood sugar levels.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Public Awareness:</strong><ul><li>Schools and communities should educate people about NCD risks.</li></ul></li></ol><h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Management of NCDs</strong></h1><ul><li><strong>Diabetes:</strong> Use insulin injections, monitor blood sugar.</li><li><strong>Hypertension:</strong> Take prescribed drugs, reduce salt intake.</li><li><strong>Asthma:</strong> Use inhalers, avoid triggers like dust.</li></ul><p><strong>Evaluation Questions</strong></p><ol start="1"><li>Define non-communicable diseases.</li><li>Name three NCDs common in Nigeria and their causes.</li><li>How can regular exercise prevent NCDs?</li></ol><p><strong>Projects/Activities</strong></p><ol start="1"><li><strong>Poster Making:</strong> Design a poster on "Say No to Smoking."</li><li><strong>Role Play:</strong> Act out a scene where a friend encourages another to eat healthy.</li></ol>`
